sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: A second bimedial straight line is divided at one point only.
----
proof:

Let {AB} be a second bimedial straight line divided at {C}, so that {AC} and {CB} are medial straight lines commensurable in square only and containing a medial rectangle. It is then manifest that {C} is not at the point of bisection, because the segments are not commensurable in length. [!just X.38]

I say that {AB} is not so divided at another point.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="350" height="200" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="X.44 diagram" src="propX44.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Joyce's figure: AB divided at C and, on the supposition, at D; the
    // rational line EF with the rectangle EK (F,E,N,K, random face) and the
    // strip GHML between the applied rectangles EG and EL. Added, all
    // invisible at rest: transparent targets AC, CB, AD, DB, EN, EH, HN, EM,
    // MN (;0;0;0); faceless, strokeless rectangles EK, EG, HK, EL, MK
    // (;0;0;0;0) that carry the highlights, since the filled ones would be
    // raised over the figure (#140); solid initiallyHidden twins EF, GH, ML
    // so the construction strokes EF from E and the sides from H and M.
