              highlighted: ["EL","AD","DB"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "M",    name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "ML",   name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "GHML", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill } ] } },
            { text: "which were proved less than the sum of the squares on {AC} and {CB}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["EL","EG"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "X.41.Lemma" }] },
            { text: "Then the remainder {MK} also equals twice the rectangle {AD} by {DB}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["MK","AD","DB"] },
            { text: "Now, since the squares on {AC} and {CB} are medial, therefore {EG} is medial.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["AC","CB","EG"] },
            { text: "And it is applied to the rational straight line {EF}, therefore {EH} is rational and incommensurable in length with {EF}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["EG","EF","EH"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "EH rational, incommensurable in length with EF", ref: "X.22" }] },
            { text: "For the same reason {HN} is also rational and incommensurable in length with {EF}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["HK","HN","EF"] },
            { text: "And, since {AC} and {CB} are medial straight lines commensurable in square only,",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["AC","CB"] },
            { text: "therefore {AC} is incommensurable in length with {CB}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["AC","CB"] },
            { text: "But {AC} is to {CB} as the square on {AC} is to the rectangle {AC} by {CB},",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["AC","CB"] },
            { text: "therefore the square on {AC} is incommensurable with the rectangle {AC} by {CB}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["AC","CB"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "AC² incommensurable with AC·CB", ref: "X.11" }] },
            { text: "But the sum of the squares on {AC} and {CB} is commensurable with the square on {AC},",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["AC","CB"] },
            { text: "for {AC} and {CB} are commensurable in square.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["AC","CB"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "AC² + CB² commensurable with AC²", ref: "X.15" }] },
            { text: "And twice the rectangle {AC} by {CB} is commensurable with the rectangle {AC} by {CB}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["AC","CB"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "2 AC·CB commensurable with AC·CB", ref: "X.6" }] },
            { text: "Therefore the sum of the squares on {AC} and {CB} is also incommensurable with twice the rectangle {AC} by {CB}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["AC","CB"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "AC² + CB² incommensurable with 2 AC·CB", ref: "X.13" }] },
            { text: "But {EG} equals the sum of the squares on {AC} and {CB}, and {HK} equals twice the rectangle {AC} by {CB},",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["EG","HK"] },
            { text: "therefore {EG} is incommensurable with {HK}, so that {EH} is also incommensurable in length with {HN}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["EG","HK","EH","HN"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "EG : HK = EH : HN", ref: "VI.1" },
                               { claim: "EH, HN incommensurable in length", ref: "X.11" }] },
            { text: "And they are rational, therefore {EH} and {HN} are rational straight lines commensurable in square only.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["EH","HN"] },
            { text: "But, if two rational straight lines commensurable in square only are added together,",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["EH","HN"] },
            { text: "then the whole is the irrational which is called binomial.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["EN"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "X.36" }] },
            { text: "Therefore {EN} is a binomial straight line divided at {H}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["EN","H"] },
            { text: "In the same way {EM} and {MN} is also proved to be rational straight lines commensurable in square only,",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["EM","MN"] },
            { text: "and {EN} is a binomial straight line divided at different points, {H} and {M}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["EN","H","M"] },
            { text: "And {EH} is not the same with {MN},",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["EH","MN"] },
            { text: "for the sum of the squares on {AC} and {CB} is greater than the sum of the squares on {AD} and {DB}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["EG","EL"] },
            { text: "But the sum of the squares on {AD} and {DB} is greater than twice the rectangle {AD} by {DB},",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["AD","DB"] },
            { text: "therefore the sum of the squares on {AC} and {CB}, that is, {EG}, is much greater",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["EG"] },
            { text: "than twice the rectangle {AD} by {DB}, that is, {MK}, so that {EH} is also greater than {MN}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["MK","EH","MN"] },
            { text: "Therefore {EH} is not the same with {MN}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["EH","MN"] },
            { text: "Therefore, a second bimedial straight line is divided at one point only.",
              visible: SET,
              highlighted: ["AB","C"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            if (ref === "X.44") return "#prop";
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "II.4":       "/elements/books/bookII/propositions/propII4/",
        "VI.1":       "/elements/books/bookVI/propositions/propVI1/",
        "X.6":        "/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX6/",
        "X.11":       "/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX11/",
        "X.13":       "/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX13/",
        "X.15":       "/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX15/",
        "X.22":       "/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX22/",
        "X.36":       "/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX36/",
        "X.38":       "/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX38/",
        "X.41.Lemma": "/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX41/#lemma"
    });
</script>
</figure>

<div class="just"><a href="/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX41/#lemma">X.41.Lemma</a></div>

If possible, let it also be divided at {D}, so that {AC} is not the same with {DB}, but {AC} is supposed greater. It is then clear that the sum of the squares on {AD} and {DB} is also, as we proved above, less than the sum of the squares on {AC} and {CB}. Suppose that {AD} and {DB} are medial straight lines commensurable in square only and containing a medial rectangle.

Set out a rational straight line {EF}, apply to {EF} the rectangular parallelogram {EK} equal to the square on {AB}, and subtract {EG}, equal to the sum of the squares on {AC} and {CB}. Then the remainder {HK} equals twice the rectangle {AC} by {CB}. [!just II.4]

<div class="just"><a href="/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX41/#lemma">X.41.Lemma</a></div>

Again, subtract {EL}, equal to the sum of the squares on {AD} and {DB}, which were proved less than the sum of the squares on {AC} and {CB}. Then the remainder {MK} also equals twice the rectangle {AD} by {DB}.

Now, since the squares on {AC} and {CB} are medial, therefore {EG} is medial.

And it is applied to the rational straight line {EF}, therefore {EH} is rational and incommensurable in length with {EF}. [!just X.22]

For the same reason {HN} is also rational and incommensurable in length with {EF}.

And, since {AC} and {CB} are medial straight lines commensurable in square only, therefore {AC} is incommensurable in length with {CB}.

But {AC} is to {CB} as the square on {AC} is to the rectangle {AC} by {CB}, therefore the square on {AC} is incommensurable with the rectangle {AC} by {CB}. [!just X.11]

But the sum of the squares on {AC} and {CB} is commensurable with the square on {AC}, for {AC} and {CB} are commensurable in square. [!just X.15]

And twice the rectangle {AC} by {CB} is commensurable with the rectangle {AC} by {CB}. [!just X.6]

Therefore the sum of the squares on {AC} and {CB} is also incommensurable with twice the rectangle {AC} by {CB}. [!just X.13]

But {EG} equals the sum of the squares on {AC} and {CB}, and {HK} equals twice the rectangle {AC} by {CB}, therefore {EG} is incommensurable with {HK}, so that {EH} is also incommensurable in length with {HN}. [!just VI.1; X.11]

And they are rational, therefore {EH} and {HN} are rational straight lines commensurable in square only.

But, if two rational straight lines commensurable in square only are added together, then the whole is the irrational which is called binomial. [!just X.36]

Therefore {EN} is a binomial straight line divided at {H}.

In the same way {EM} and {MN} is also proved to be rational straight lines commensurable in square only, and {EN} is a binomial straight line divided at different points, {H} and {M}.

And {EH} is not the same with {MN}, for the sum of the squares on {AC} and {CB} is greater than the sum of the squares on {AD} and {DB}.

But the sum of the squares on {AD} and {DB} is greater than twice the rectangle {AD} by {DB}, therefore the sum of the squares on {AC} and {CB}, that is, {EG}, is much greater than twice the rectangle {AD} by {DB}, that is, {MK}, so that {EH} is also greater than {MN}.

Therefore {EH} is not the same with {MN}.

Therefore, *a second bimedial straight line is divided at one point only.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
